e\n(1,1)-(1,2) & \\infty \\ (\\text{vertical})\\\\\n(1,1)-(2,1) & 0 \\ (\\text{horizontal})\\\\\n(1,1)-(1,3) & \\infty\\\\\n(1,1)-(3,1) & 0\\\\\n(1,1)-(2,2) & 1 \\ (\\text{sunny})\\\\\n(1,1)-(2,2) & 1\\\\\n(1,2)-(2,1) & -1\\\\\n(1,2)-(1,3) & \\infty\\\\\n(1,2)-(3,1) & -\\tfrac12 \\ (\\text{sunny})\\\\\n(1,2)-(2,2) & 0\\\\\n(2,1)-(1,3) & -2 \\ (\\text{sunny})\\\\\n(2,1)-(3,1) & 0\\\\\n(2,1)-(2,2) & \\infty\\\\\n(1,3)-(3,1) & -1\\\\\n(1,3)-(2,2) & -1\\\\\n(3,1)-(2,2) & -1\\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThe only pairs whose slope is **not** \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\qquad \\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\qquad \\{(2,1),(1,3)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus any sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\), and the three listed lines are the only sunny lines that contain two points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Counting argument\n\nWe must use exactly three distinct lines, two of them sunny.  \nSince each sunny line can cover at most two points, the two sunny lines together can cover at most four points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).  \nTherefore the third (non\u2011sunny) line must contain **at least two** points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Possible non\u2011sunny lines\n\nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(y=c\\)), the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(x=c\\)), or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. \\(x+y=d\\)).  \nAmong the points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) the only such lines that contain two or more points are:\n\n* Horizontal lines: \\(y=1\\) (contains \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\)), \\(y=2\\) (contains \\((1,2),(2,2)\\)).\n* Vertical lines: \\(x=1\\) (contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\)), \\(x=2\\) (contains \\((2,1),(2,2)\\)).\n* Diagonal lines of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=3\\) (contains \\((1,2),(2,1)\\)), \\(x+y=4\\) (contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\)).\n\nWe examine each possibility.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Exhaustive case analysis  \n\n| Non\u2011sunny line \\(L_{ns}\\) | Points on \\(L_{ns}\\) | Remaining points \\(\\mathcal{R}\\) | Can \\(\\mathcal{R}\\) be covered by two sunny lines? |\n|---------------------------|----------------------|----------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|\n| \\(y=1\\)                   | \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) | \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\)            | Any line through two of these three points is either vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) (see \u00a71). Hence no sunny line can contain two of them; two sunny lines could cover at most two points, leaving one uncovered. |\n| \\(x=1\\)                   | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) | \\((2,1),(3,1),(2,2)\\)            | Same reasoning as above. |\n| \\(x+y=4\\)                 | \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\)            | Any pair among the three remaining points yields a vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) line (see \u00a71). Thus no sunny line can contain two of them. |\n| \\(y=2\\)                   | \\((1,2),(2,2)\\)       | \\((1,1),(2,1),(1,3),(3,1)\\)      | The point \\((1,1)\\) cannot be paired with any other remaining point by a sunny line (all such pairs are vertical or horizontal). Hence at least three sunny lines would be needed. |\n| \\(x=2\\)                   | \\((2,1),(2,2)\\)       | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\)      | Same obstruction as the previous row. |\n| \\(x+y=3\\)                 | \\((1,2),(2,1)\\)       | \\((1,1),(2,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\)      | The only sunny pair among the remaining points is \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)). After covering them, the two points \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\), which is non\u2011sunny. Hence a second sunny line cannot cover both; a third sunny line would be required. |\n\nIn every possible choice for the non\u2011sunny line, the set of uncovered points cannot be covered by only two sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nSince every admissible non\u2011sunny line leads to a contradiction, no arrangement of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines can satisfy the covering condition for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3,\\;k=2\\text{ is impossible}.}\n\\]"
