\frac{n+1}{2}\\) points.\nThe only slope that attains the bound \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) is \\(1\\); a line of\nslope \\(1\\) meets the triangle in the points \\((t,t)\\) with\n\\(1\\le t\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\).  There are precisely \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) distinct\nsuch lines, far fewer than the required \\(n\\) distinct lines.  Hence a covering\nby \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot exist when \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) we have \\(m_3=2\\) and \\(3\\cdot m_3=6=|T_3|\\); the bound does not\nexclude the case \\(k=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=c,\\qquad c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (non‑sunny) and each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies\non the line with \\(c=a+b\\).  Thus \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Inductive construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for every integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Base \\(n=3\\).*  \nExplicit configurations are:\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three lines \\(x+y=2,3,4\\);\n* \\(k=1\\): the sunny line \\(y=x\\) together with the two lines\n  \\(x+y=3,4\\);\n* \\(k=2\\): the sunny lines \\(y=x\\) and \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\) together\n  with the line \\(x+y=4\\);\n* \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines\n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\n  \\]\n  which were verified point‑by‑point in the detailed solution.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement true for some \\(n-1\\ge3\\).  \nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{0}: x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{0}\\) is non‑sunny and contains exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  The set\n\\(T_n\\setminus L_{0}\\) is precisely \\(T_{n-1}\\).  By the induction hypothesis,\nfor any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le (n-1)-2=n-3\\) we can cover \\(T_{n-1}\\) with\n\\(n-1\\) lines having exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.  Adding the line \\(L_{0}\\) yields a\ncover of \\(T_n\\) with \\(n\\) lines and the same number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\nThus every \\(k\\le n-3\\) is realizable for \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) (all but one line sunny)\n\nAgain use the non‑sunny line \\(L_{0}\\) from (2).  The remaining points are\n\\(T_{n-1}\\).  Pair the points of \\(T_{n-1}\\) as follows:\n\n\\[\n(1,1)\\!-\\!(2,2),\\;\n(1,2)\\!-\\!(3,1),\\;\n(1,3)\\!-\\!(4,1),\\;\\dots ,\\;\n\\bigl(1,n-1\\bigr)\\!-\\!\\bigl(n-1,1\\bigr).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach pair determines a line whose slope is respectively  \n\n\\[\n1,\\;-\\tfrac12,\\;-\\tfrac13,\\dots ,\\;-\\tfrac1{\\,n-2\\,},\n\\]\n\nnone of which equals \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence all are sunny.\nThe lines are distinct because they have different slopes.  Together with\n\\(L_{0}\\) we obtain \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of them sunny, that\ncover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Summary of attainable values of \\(k\\)\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) the constructions of Section 4 give \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).\n* For every \\(n\\ge4\\):\n  * \\(k=0\\) is given by Section 3;\n  * \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) follows from the induction of Section 4;\n  * \\(k=n-1\\) is provided by the explicit pairing of Section 5;\n  * \\(k=n\\) is impossible by the argument of Section 2.\n\nHence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification 