point}&L_{s}&L_{v}&L_{h}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1)&\\checkmark&\\checkmark&\\checkmark\\\\\n(1,2)&&\\checkmark&\\\\\n(1,3)&&\\checkmark&\\\\\n(2,1)&&&\\checkmark\\\\\n(2,2)&\\checkmark&&\\\\\n(3,1)&&&\\checkmark\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  \\(k=2\\) (exactly two sunny lines) – impossibility\n\nAssume a configuration with two sunny lines and one non‑sunny line exists.\nA non‑sunny line must be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently it is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c .\n\\]\n\n*Maximum number of points covered by a non‑sunny line.*  \nFrom \\(\\mathcal P\\) we see:\n\n* a vertical line \\(x=c\\) can contain at most three points (for \\(c=1\\));\n* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) can contain at most three points (for \\(c=1\\));\n* a line \\(x+y=c\\) can contain at most three points (for \\(c=4\\)).\n\nHence after removing the points covered by the non‑sunny line, at least three points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) remain uncovered.\n\nConsider each possible non‑sunny line that attains the maximal coverage of three points.\n\n| non‑sunny line | points it covers | remaining points |\n|----------------|------------------|------------------|\n| \\(x=1\\)        | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) | \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) |\n| \\(y=1\\)        | \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) | \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\) |\n| \\(x+y=4\\)      | \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) |\n\nIn each of the three rows the three *remaining* points are pairwise aligned only in forbidden directions:\n\n* For the first row, the pairs \\((2,1)-(2,2)\\) are vertical, \\((2,1)-(3,1)\\) are horizontal, and \\((2,2)-(3,1)\\) have slope \\(-1\\).\n* For the second row, the pairs \\((1,2)-(1,3)\\) are vertical, \\((1,2)-(2,2)\\) are horizontal, and \\((1,3)-(2,2)\\) have slope \\(-1\\).\n* For the third row, the pairs \\((1,1)-(1,2)\\) are vertical, \\((1,1)-(2,1)\\) are horizontal, and \\((1,2)-(2,1)\\) have slope \\(-1\\).\n\nThus **any line through any two of the remaining points is non‑sunny**.  \nConsequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three remaining points.  \nWith only two sunny lines we can cover at most two of those three points, leaving at least one point uncovered – a contradiction.\n\nIf the non‑sunny line covers fewer than three points (e.g. \\(x=2\\) or \\(y=2\\) or \\(x+y=3\\)), then at least four points remain. Two sunny lines can each contain at most two of them, but among the four points there is always at least one point that is isolated in the sense that any line through it and another remaining point is non‑sunny (the same reasoning as above). Hence the four points cannot be covered by only two sunny lines.  \n\nTherefore a configuration with exactly two sunny lines **cannot exist** for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  \\(k=3\\) (all three lines sunny)\n\nConsider the three sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:\\;y=x &&\\text{slope }1,\\\\\nL_{2}&:\\;y=-\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac52 &&\\text{through }(1,2),(3,1),\\\\\nL_{3}&:\\;y=-2x+5 &&\\text{through }(1,3),(2,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll three slopes (\\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\)) differ from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each line is sunny and the three lines are distinct.\n\nVerification:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\n\\text{point}&L_{1}&L_{2}&L_{3}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1)&\\checkmark&&\\\\\n(2,2)&\\checkmark&&\\\\\n(1,2)&&\\checkmark&\\\\\n(3,1)&&\\checkmark&\\\\\n(1,3)&&&\\checkmark\\\\\n(2,1)&&&\\checkmark\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on at least one of the three sunny lines, so a configuration with \\(k=3\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion for \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe exhaustive analysis yields\n\n\\[\nk=0\\ \\text{possible},\\qquad\nk=1\\ \\text{possible},\\qquad\nk=2\\ \\text{impossible},\\qquad\nk=3\\ \\text{possible}.\n\\]\n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}} .\n\\] ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
