e \\(s=2\\); replace the line \\(x+y=2\\) by any sunny line passing through the single point \\((1,1)\\) (for instance \\(y=x\\)).  \n   The resulting family consists of one sunny line and \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny diagonal lines, still exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, and it covers all points.\n\n5. **The special case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   Here the diagonals are  \n\n   \\[\n   D_2=\\{(1,1)\\},\\; D_3=\\{(1,2),(2,1)\\},\\; D_4=\\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Their sizes are \\(1,2,3\\).  \n   Because the largest diagonal contains only three points, we may replace **each** diagonal by a sunny line that passes through all its points (a line of slope \\(1\\) for \\(D_2\\), a line of slope \\(-2\\) for \\(D_3\\), and a line of slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) for \\(D_4\\)).  \n   Thus we obtain a configuration with three sunny lines.  \n   Together with the constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) (as described above) we get the admissible values \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\) when \\(n=3\\).\n\n6. **Impossibility for \\(k\\ge 2\\) when \\(n\\ge 4\\).**  \n   Suppose \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines exist.  \n   Because each sunny line meets every diagonal in at most one point, the total number of points of the diagonals that are *not* used as lines is at most \\(k\\) per diagonal.  \n   The diagonal \\(D_3\\) already contains two points; covering both of them would require two distinct sunny lines.  \n   The diagonal \\(D_4\\) contains three points, which would need three distinct sunny lines, and so on.  \n   Since we have only \\(k\\) sunny lines, we cannot replace any diagonal larger than size \\(k\\).  \n   For \\(n\\ge 4\\) the diagonal \\(D_4\\) has size \\(3>k\\) whenever \\(k\\le 2\\); therefore at least one diagonal of size \\(\\ge 3\\) must be kept as a line, forcing the number of sunny lines to be at most one.  \n   Hence \\(k\\ge 2\\) is impossible for all \\(n\\ge 4\\).\n\nAll cases are now covered, establishing the claimed set of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n---------------------------