3,\\dots ,n+1).\n   \\]\n\n   All of them are non\u2011sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Keep the diagonal lines for all \\(s\\neq2\\) and replace the line \\(x+y=2\\) by any sunny line through the single point \\((1,1)\\) (for instance \\(y=x\\)).  \n   The family consists of one sunny line and \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny diagonal lines, still exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, and it covers all points.\n\n5. **General construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) ( \\(n\\ge4\\) ).**  \n\n   *Idea.*  Keep the **largest** diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) (the line \\(x+y=n+1\\)) as a non\u2011sunny line.  \n   Replace the remaining \\(n-1\\) diagonals \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_n\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines; the other \\(n-1-k\\) of those diagonals will stay as diagonal lines.  \n\n   *Step\u2011by\u2011step.*  \n\n   1. Choose \\(k\\) distinct integers  \n\n      \\[\n      t_1,t_2,\\dots ,t_k\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\n      \\]\n\n      (these are the indices of the diagonals that will be replaced).  \n\n   2. For each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) pick a point  \n\n      \\[\n      P_j=(1,t_j-1)\\in D_{t_j}\n      \\]\n\n      (the left\u2011most point of the chosen diagonal).  \n\n   3. For the same \\(j\\) pick a point  \n\n      \\[\n      Q_j=(j,\\,n+1-j)\\in D_{n+1}\n      \\]\n\n      (different points of the largest diagonal for different \\(j\\)).  \n\n   4. Define the line  \n\n      \\[\n      L_j:=\\overline{P_jQ_j}.\n      \\]\n\n      Its slope is  \n\n      \\[\n      m_j=\\frac{(n+1-j)-(t_j-1)}{j-1}\n          =\\frac{n-t_j+2-j}{j-1}.\n      \\]\n\n      Because \\(j\\ge2\\) we have \\(j-1\\neq0\\); the numerator is never \\(0\\) (otherwise \\(t_j=n+2\\), impossible) and it is never \\(-(j-1)\\) (which would give slope \\(-1\\)).  \n      Hence \\(m_j\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently each \\(L_j\\) is **sunny**.  \n\n   5. The set  \n\n      \\[\n      \\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\{x+y=n+1\\}\\cup\n                 \\{x+y=s\\mid s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{t_1,\\dots ,t_k\\}\\}\n      \\]\n\n      consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny diagonal lines, i.e. of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n   *Verification.*  \n\n   - Every point of the kept diagonals \\(x+y=s\\) (with \\(s\\neq t_j\\)) lies on its own diagonal line.  \n   - For each replaced diagonal \\(D_{t_j}\\) the point \\(P_j\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_j\\); the other points of that diagonal are covered by the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (they all belong to the largest diagonal after adding the appropriate \\(x\\)\u2011coordinate).  \n   - The line \\(x+y=n+1\\) covers **all** points of the largest diagonal, in particular the points \\(Q_j\\) used to define the sunny lines.  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n   Because the construction works for **any** choice of the \\(k\\) indices \\(t_j\\), we obtain a configuration for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n6. **The special case \\(n=3\\).**  \n\n   The set of points is  \n\n   \\[\n   D_2=\\{(1,1)\\},\\;\n   D_3=\\{(1,2),(2,1)\\},\\;\n   D_4=\\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *\\(k=0\\)* and *\\(k=1\\)* are obtained as in step\u202f4.  \n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  Take the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\n   \\]\n\n   which respectively contain the point sets \\(\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\}\\), \\(\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\}\\) and \\(\\{(2,1),(1,3)\\}\\).  \n   Together they cover all nine points of \\(T_3\\).  \n\n   *\\(k=2\\) is impossible.*  \n   To replace the diagonal \\(D_3\\) (which has two points) we need **two** distinct sunny lines, because the two points of \\(D_3\\) lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)) and therefore cannot be placed on the same sunny line.  \n   The remaining point \\((1,1)\\) of \\(D_2\\) would require a third sunny line or a diagonal line, but we are allowed only two sunny lines in total.  \n   Hence no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\n\n7. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n\n   Suppose all \\(n\\) lines were sunny.  \n   The diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) contains \\(n\\) points; a sunny line can meet this diagonal in at most one point, so the \\(n\\) sunny lines would have to contain **exactly one** point of \\(D_{n+1}\\) each.  \n   Consequently each of those lines already uses its unique intersection with \\(D_{n+1}\\); it cannot contain any other point of \\(D_{n+1}\\) and, because a sunny line meets any other diagonal in at most one point, the remaining \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\) points of the smaller diagonals would require more than the available \\(n\\) lines.  \n   This contradiction shows that \\(k=n\\) can never be achieved.\n\nAll cases are now covered, establishing the complete list of admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
