eq0\\); the numerator is never\u202f\\(0\\) (otherwise\u202f\\(t_j=n+2\\), impossible) and it is never\u202f\\(-(j-1)\\) (which would give slope\u202f\\(-1\\)). Hence\u202f\\(m_j\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap:**  \nThe statement \u201cthe numerator is never\u202f\\(-(j-1)\\)\u201d is asserted without verification.  One must check that for all admissible\u202f\\(j\\) (with\u202f\\(2\\le j\\le k\\le n-1\\)) and all admissible\u202f\\(t_j\\) (with\u202f\\(2\\le t_j\\le n\\)) we indeed have  \n\\[\nn-t_j+2-j\\neq -(j-1)\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; n-t_j+2\\neq -1\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; t_j\\neq n+3,\n\\]  \nwhich holds because \\(t_j\\le n\\).  Although the conclusion is true, the proof is omitted, so there is a small gap in rigor.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f6 \u2013 The special case\u202f\\(n=3\\)\n\n> \u201c*\\(k=0\\)* and *\\(k=1\\)* are obtained as in step\u202f4.  \n>   \n> *\\(k=3\\).*  Take the three sunny lines  \n> \\[\n> y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\n> \\]  \n> which respectively contain the point sets \\(\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\}\\), \\(\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\}\\) and \\(\\{(2,1),(1,3)\\}\\).  \n> Together they cover all nine points of\u202f\\(T_3\\).  \n>   \n> *\\(k=2\\) is impossible.*  \n> To replace the diagonal\u202f\\(D_3\\) (which has two points) we need **two** distinct sunny lines, because the two points of\u202f\\(D_3\\) lie on a line of slope\u202f\\(-1\\) (parallel to\u202f\\(x+y=0\\)) and therefore cannot be placed on the same sunny line.  \n> The remaining point \\((1,1)\\) of\u202f\\(D_2\\) would require a third sunny line or a diagonal line, but we are allowed only two sunny lines in total.  \n> Hence no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\u201d\n\n**Verification of the constructions:**  \n- The three lines listed are indeed sunny (slopes\u202f\\(1,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\,-2\\) are none of\u202f\\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  Substituting the coordinates shows each line contains the claimed pair of points