This gives a configuration with exactly one sunny line, so \\(k=1\\) is always possible.\n\n3. **The special case \\(n=3\\).**  \n\n   The points are  \n\n   \\[\n   D_2=\\{(1,1)\\},\\;\n   D_3=\\{(1,2),(2,1)\\},\\;\n   D_4=\\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *\\(k=3\\) construction.*  \n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n   \\]\n\n   contain respectively  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1\\cap T_3=\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\;\n   L_2\\cap T_3=\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\;\n   L_3\\cap T_3=\\{(2,1),(1,3)\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which together cover all nine points of \\(T_3\\).  \n   Hence \\(k=3\\) is attainable for \\(n=3\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\) when \\(n\\ge 4\\).**  \n\n   *Lemma.*  \n   If a diagonal \\(D_s\\) with \\(|D_s|\\ge 2\\) is omitted from the family \\(\\mathcal D\\), then at least \\(|D_s|\\) sunny lines are required to cover the points of \\(D_s\\).\n\n   *Proof of Lemma.*  \n   Any non‑sunny line is parallel either to the axes or to the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   All diagonals \\(D_t\\) are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); consequently a non‑sunny line that is not a diagonal cannot meet a diagonal \\(D_s\\) at any point, and two distinct diagonals are parallel, so they never intersect.  \n   Therefore the only lines that can contain points of \\(D_s\\) are the diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) itself (non‑sunny) and sunny lines.  \n   A sunny line is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); hence it meets the diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) in **at most one** point.  \n   Consequently covering all \\(|D_s|\\) points of \\(D_s\\) without using the diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) needs at least \\(|D_s|\\) distinct sunny lines. ∎\n\n   For \\(n\\ge 4\\) the smallest diagonal with more than one point is \\(D_3\\) (it has two points).  \n   By the Lemma, if we omit \\(D_3\\) we would need at least two sunny lines just for its two points.  \n   Moreover the diagonal \\(D_4\\) (three points) would require at least three sunny lines if omi