es at most one additional line, so (1) yields \\(k\\le1\\).  \n\n   Hence for every \\(n\\ge4\\) we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n5. **The special case \\(n=3\\).**  \n\n   The required points are  \n\n   \\[\n   D_2=\\{(1,1)\\},\\;\n   D_3=\\{(1,2),(2,1)\\},\\;\n   D_4=\\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *\\(k=3\\) construction.*  \n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n   \\]\n\n   are pairwise distinct and have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n   Their intersections with \\(T_3\\) are\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1\\cap T_3 &=\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\\\\n   L_2\\cap T_3 &=\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\\\\n   L_3\\cap T_3 &=\\{(2,1),(1,3)\\}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Together they contain all six points of \\(T_3\\); thus a configuration with \\(k=3\\) exists.\n\n   *Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).*  \n   As in Lemma 1, the two points of \\(D_3\\) cannot lie on a single sunny line (the line through them has slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Consequently any covering of \\(D_3\\) without using the diagonal \\(x+y=3\\) needs **at least two** distinct non‑diagonal lines.  \n   If we have exactly two sunny lines, the remaining \\(n-2=1\\) line must be a diagonal; but then at most one of the three diagonals can be omitted, leaving at least one point of \\(D_3\\) uncovered – a contradiction.  \n   Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible for \\(n=3\\).\n\n6. **Collecting the results.**  \n\n   * For every \\(n\\ge3\\) we have constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).  \n   * For \\(n=3\\) we additionally have a construction for \\(k=3\\) and we have proved that \\(k=2\\) cannot occur.  \n   * For \\(n\\ge4\\) Lemma 1 forces the omission of at most the diagonal \\(D_2\\); therefore at most one sunny line can appear, i.e. \\(k\\le1\\).  \n\n   Hence the complete list of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the boxed answer.\n\n------