f \\(L\\) is \\((b_2-b_1)/(a_2-a_1)=-1\\); hence \\(L\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny. \u220e\n\n   Consequently, to cover all points of a diagonal \\(D_s\\) **without** using the diagonal itself we need at least \\(|D_s|\\) distinct non\u2011diagonal lines (each can supply at most one point of \\(D_s\\)).  \n\n   Suppose a configuration uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Since there are \\(n\\) diagonals in total, at most \\(n-k\\) of them can be kept; the remaining \\(k\\) diagonals are omitted and must be covered by the other \\(k\\) lines (the sunny ones).  \n   Let \\(O\\) be the set of omitted diagonals.  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;\\ge\\;\\max_{s\\in O}(s-1). \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge4\\) the smallest diagonal with more than one point is \\(D_3\\) (size \\(2\\)).  \n   If \\(D_3\\in O\\) then \\(\\max_{s\\in O}(s-1)\\ge2\\) and (1) forces \\(k\\ge2\\).  \n   But we would then have **only** the two sunny lines to replace the omitted diagonals, and each of those two lines can meet \\(D_3\\) in at most one point.  \n   Hence the two points of \\(D_3\\) could not both be covered \u2013 a contradiction.  \n\n   Therefore \\(D_3\\) cannot be omitted; the only diagonal that may be omitted is \\(D_2\\) (which consists of the single point \\((1,1)\\)).  \n   Omitting \\(D_2\\) requires at most one additional line, so (1) yields \\(k\\le1\\).  \n\n   Hence for every \\(n\\ge4\\) we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n5. **The special case \\(n=3\\).**  \n\n   The required points are  \n\n   \\[\n   D_2=\\{(1,1)\\},\\;\n   D_3=\\{(1,2),(2,1)\\},\\;\n   D_4=\\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *\\(k=3\\) construction.*  \n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n   \\]\n\n   are pairwise distinct and have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n   Their intersections with \\(T_3