"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1\\},   & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   For \\(s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   D_s=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=s\\},\n   \\qquad |D_s|=s-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The set of points that must be covered is  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\bigcup_{s=2}^{n+1}D_s ,\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   A line is *non\u2011sunny* iff it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is *sunny*.\n\n2. **All\u2011diagonal construction ( \\(k=0\\) ).**  \n   The \\(n\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n   \\]\n\n   are pairwise distinct, non\u2011sunny and each contains exactly the points of \\(D_s\\).  \n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of them and we have a configuration with \\(k=0\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n3. **One\u2011sunny construction ( \\(k=1\\) ).**  \n   Replace the diagonal \\(x+y=2\\) (which contains only \\((1,1)\\)) by any sunny line through \\((1,1)\\); for instance  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;y=x .\n   \\]\n\n   Keep the remaining diagonals \\(x+y=s\\;(s=3,\\dots ,n+1)\\).  \n   The new family still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines and covers all points of \\(T_n\\); therefore \\(k=1\\) is always attainable.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n\n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   Let \\(D_s\\) be a diagonal with \\(|D_s|=s-1\\ge2\\).  \n   Any line that is **not** the diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) meets \\(D_s\\) in **at most one** point (because two distinct points of \\(D_s\\) determine the line \\(x+y=s\\), which has slope \\(-1\\) and is non\u2011sunny).  \n\n   *Proof.*  If a line \\(L\\) contains two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in D_s\\) then  \n   \\(a_1+b_1=a_2+b_2=s\\) and the slope of \\(L\\) is \\((b_2-b_1)/(a_2-a_1)=-1\\); hence \\(L\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny. \u220e\n\n   Consequently, to cover all points of a diagonal \\(D_s\\) **without** using the diagonal itself we need at least \\(|D_s|\\) distinct non\u2011diagonal lines (each can supply at most one point of \\(D_s\\)).  \n\n   Suppose a configuration uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Since there are \\(n\\) diagonals in total, at most \\(n-k\\) of them can be kept; the remaining \\(k\\) diagonals are omitted and must be covered by the other \\(k\\) lines (the sunny ones).  \n   Let \\(O\\) be the set of omitted diagonals.  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;\\ge\\;\\max_{s\\in O}(s-1). \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge4\\) the smallest diagonal with more than one point is \\(D_3\\) (size \\(2\\)).  \n   If \\(D_3\\in O\\) then \\(\\max_{s\\in O}(s-1)\\ge2\\) and (1) forces \\(k\\ge2\\).  \n   But we would then have **only** the two sunny lines to replace the omitted diagonals, and each of those two lines can meet \\(D_3\\) in at most one point.  \n   Hence the two points of \\(D_3\\) could not both be covered \u2013 a contradiction.  \n\n   Therefore \\(D_3\\) cannot be omitted; the only diagonal that may be omitted is \\(D_2\\) (which consists of the single point \\((1,1)\\)).  \n   Omitting \\(D_2\\) requires at most one additional line, so (1) yields \\(k\\le1\\).  \n\n   Hence for every \\(n\\ge4\\) we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n5. **The special case \\(n=3\\).**  \n\n   The required points are  \n\n   \\[\n   D_2=\\{(1,1)\\},\\;\n   D_3=\\{(1,2),(2,1)\\},\\;\n   D_4=\\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *\\(k=3\\) construction.*  \n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n   \\]\n\n   are pairwise distinct and have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n   Their intersections with \\(T_3\\) are\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1\\cap T_3 &=\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\\\\n   L_2\\cap T_3 &=\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\\\\n   L_3\\cap T_3 &=\\{(2,1),(1,3)\\}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Together they contain all six points of \\(T_3\\); thus a configuration with \\(k=3\\) exists.\n\n   *Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).*  \n   As in Lemma\u202f1, the two points of \\(D_3\\) cannot lie on a single sunny line (the line through them has slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Consequently any covering of \\(D_3\\) without using the diagonal \\(x+y=3\\) needs **at least two** distinct non\u2011diagonal lines.  \n   If we have exactly two sunny lines, the remaining \\(n-2=1\\) line must be a diagonal; but then at most one of the three diagonals can be omitted, leaving at least one point of \\(D_3\\) uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.  \n   Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible for \\(n=3\\).\n\n6. **Collecting the results.**  \n\n   * For every \\(n\\ge3\\) we have constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).  \n   * For \\(n=3\\) we additionally have a construction for \\(k=3\\) and we have proved that \\(k=2\\) cannot occur.  \n   * For \\(n\\ge4\\) Lemma\u202f1 forces the omission of at most the diagonal \\(D_2\\); therefore at most one sunny line can appear, i.e. \\(k\\le1\\).  \n\n   Hence the complete list of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the boxed answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1\\},   & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
